# Asserted test-count floors — ONE definition, sourced by every gate.
#
# These scripts assert the COUNT, not the exit code, because a runner whose glob
# matches nothing exits 0 having run zero tests. That design only works if the
# numbers are right, and they had drifted: the same Kotlin floor was written in
# two places (one as a hand-summed total), and the appd floor was >0 in the script
# that puts the DAEMON into production while the desktop release demanded >=300 —
# so the production path accepted a suite that had lost 90% of its tests.
#
# Raise a floor here when tests are added; never edit a copy in a script.

KOTLIN_SHARED_MIN=1191   # :core jvm + :core android + :app + :ui jvm   (actual 1196 on 2026-08-27)
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the session cost estimate: 9 in :core, so they count
# twice. All of them are about a number that is renderable and wrong — the money
# formatter (a four-figure estimate has to group its thousands the way the plan
# card's amounts do, or one app prints money two ways), and the three honesty
# rules the daemon's estimate is only worth showing under: something too small to
# show is "<$0.01" rather than "$0.00" that claims it cost nothing, EXACTLY
# nothing is "$0.00" and must not read as too small to show (that is what a
# session priced entirely on unknown models gets), and no estimate at all draws
# no stat rather than a zero. Plus the decode guard against a real daemon body:
# a renamed key decodes to the default and the header quietly loses the stat,
# which is the one failure here nothing on screen would look wrong for.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the attach chooser: 7 in :ui for AttachChooser, the one
# part of that change that is not layout. Both halves of it are invisible when
# wrong — a "Notes page" row offered against a daemon with no pages route is a
# menu entry whose only outcome is a 404, and a chooser drawn over a SINGLE row
# is an extra press on the way to the only thing behind it, which is what the
# desktop's clip button becomes the moment the feature probe comes back false.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the autosave rework. All of it is :core and therefore
# counts twice: ScratchpadSaver's invariants (a fetch read before the last write
# never replaces newer text; a rev only ever lands on the content it belongs to;
# a hold belongs to a PAGE, so a failure for a page you left is still owed; the
# state line is about the page on screen; a 413 is terminal rather than a retry
# loop; a rename joins the write chain instead of racing it), the same discipline
# in SessionMetaSaver (a poll read before the save landed is ignored via a save
# generation), Watchers (two surfaces sharing one poll — closing one must not
# stop it under the other), and the page ORDER plus the daemon-shaped name-length
# rule in ScratchpadRules. Every one of them is a way work disappears with
# nothing on screen having looked wrong.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the session overview. Most of it is :core and therefore
# counts twice: GraphLayout (the map's lane allocation — the one part of that
# screen that can be wrong in a way a screenshot would not show, since two agents
# sharing a column draws one line where there are two), OverviewFormat (the
# projection card's arithmetic, plus the two house rules that keep it honest — no
# money on a per-session screen, and every estimate says it is one), and
# SessionMetaSaver (a twin of ScratchpadSaver for two fields with no revision;
# every case is a way work could be lost silently, which is the only kind of bug
# an editor with no Save button can have). Plus eight in :ui for what the map is
# SAYING with a tint — a stalled agent drawn in its own hue claims it finished.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by scratchpads. Most of it is :core and therefore counts
# twice: the naming rules and the frame a page is quoted into (ScratchpadRules —
# asserted as LITERALS, because the writer is lib/scratchpads.js in another
# language and a helper-shaped test would pass while the two sides drifted), the
# autosave state machine (ScratchpadSaver — every case is a way work could be lost
# silently), and the wire (a reference travels as an ID, and a 409 is an ANSWER
# rather than a throw). Plus the shared save line and page subtitle in :ui, and
# the phone's two new destinations.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-26 by the plan/usage rework: the countdown, the RFC 3339
# parse, the minor-unit money and the extra-usage state line moved into :core as
# PlanFormat, so their 29 tests run on BOTH targets and count twice. They are
# there because the two shells had been answering the same questions differently
# — one of them with the timezone offset sliced off the timestamp.
#
# Raised again on 2026-08-26 by Rounds polish: HuginnClient.polishRound pins the
# whole draft reaching the wire, the poll tier the call rides, and the one that
# matters most — a degraded 200 {error} decoding as an ERROR rather than throwing.
# Three tests in :core's commonTest, so they run on both targets and count twice.
#
# Raised again on 2026-08-26 by device lifecycle: agoWordsMs (:core commonTest, so
# it counts on both targets) and the "not reachable · last seen 3 days ago" line in
# the shared DevicesView — seven cases in :ui, most of them about what must NOT be
# rendered, because a bare "last seen" or a 1970 stamp is worse than no clause.
DESKTOP_EXTRA_MIN=216    # + :app-desktop, added on top of the shared floor (actual 221 on 2026-08-27)
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the keyboard and panel fixes: the Ctrl+digit leak (the
# chord typed its digit into whatever field had focus, and the page editor's
# autosave committed it), which took two tests plus the rewrite of two that were
# tautologies — `assertNull(match(...).takeIf { false })` passes against any
# behaviour at all, including the leak. Plus the three-condition predicate Esc
# and the panel toggle now share with the render sites, and the sliced backoff
# that lets a re-enabled device runner answer inside a second rather than five
# minutes.
#
# Was 189 from the day these floors were consolidated, which was ALWAYS above the
# real count — this file's own header says the numbers had drifted and one was a
# hand-summed total, and 189 was the hand-summed one. Checked 2026-08-23: no
# :app-desktop test file has been deleted since, and 14 remain, so nothing was
# lost; the number was simply never true. It never fired because the assertion is
# on the SUM (KOTLIN_SHARED_MIN + DESKTOP_EXTRA_MIN), which the shared half kept
# clearing on its own — a floor that cannot fail is not a floor.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by scratchpads: the two new palette-and-keyboard bindings
# (Ctrl+P for the pages, Ctrl+Shift+P for the panel beside a conversation), the
# palette rows for the pages themselves, and Landing learning a view that encodes
# but is deliberately NOT persistable — the rule that file states about itself.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-26 by device lifecycle: the "actual 177" above was already
# stale (the suite had reached 186 before this change), and 22 more arrived with
# it — Unenrol's whole state machine, which is where "keep the handle until the
# DELETE lands" is actually decided, plus the settings half that records the debt
# and survives a laptop being closed before the daemon could be told.
APPD_MIN=846             # server/appd node --test  (actual 851 on 2026-08-27; +18: the session cost estimate — lib/pricing rates and honesty rules 8, sessiongraph per-model accumulation 8, routes-overview wire shapes 2)
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the confirmed-findings pass over the daemon: 39 tests,
# every one of them verified by making it FAIL against the unfixed code first.
# The ones worth naming are the ones whose bug was invisible while it ran.
# lib/sessiongraph (5): `totals.errors` was emitted and never incremented, so the
# header said a run had gone fine no matter how many tool calls had failed; and an
# agent's `.meta.json` is a sibling that lands on its own schedule, so the retry
# that was supposed to pick up a late one sat BELOW the equal-size early return
# and could only run on a poll that also found new bytes — which for a finished
# agent is never. routes-overview (2): an idle session's cursor never moves, so
# the `unchanged` short-circuit is the ONLY reply a watching desktop gets — notes
# typed on the phone reached it only when the run happened to write a byte; and
# `Number(null)` is 0, which is also an empty transcript's cursor, so a first
# fetch was told nothing had changed from a state it had never seen.
# scratchpads (9 in the lib, 18 on the routes): a page whose own content holds a
# line beginning "[End scratchpad" used to close its own frame — half the page
# reached the run and the rest sat in the sender's message as raw marker text —
# now tagged on both markers with the report-tag precedent, asserted as LITERALS
# because :core carries the same bytes; a present-but-blank scratchpadId silently
# became Main; the page cap (characters) was unreachable through the body cap
# (bytes) for any three-byte script; render/<padId>.md was shared across sessions
# and rewritten per attach while its comment claimed snapshot semantics; and the
# list re-sorted itself by recency under the person typing into it. push-retire
# (3, a new file): a dead token's two halves landed up to a minute apart, and a
# restart in that minute resurrected a client row that nothing can ever drop
# again — driven end to end against a stubbed FCM, no network. pane (2): a
# terminal's own Device Attributes reply was headlining a session preview.
#
# Raised on 2026-08-27 by the session graph: lib/sessiongraph (31) walks a whole
# transcript rather than tailing it, and its two load-bearing cases are the ones
# derived empirically — usage counted ONCE PER requestId (per-record summing
# overcounted cache reads 2.7x on a real 33.8MB file) and `iterations[]` read as
# a breakdown rather than as extra spend. Both produce plausible, renderable,
# wrong numbers when they regress. routes-overview (12) covers the three routes,
# including the two that are refusals: a session with no Claude id is told no
# rather than having its notes filed under a tmux name that gets reused, and the
# graph is asserted NOT to ride the sessions list. Plus 4 in agents.test for the
# workflow blind spot (runs were scanned in one of the two places the CLI writes
# them, so a fan-out could report as no agents at all).
#
# Raised on 2026-08-26 with the plan fixture: the 2026-07-27 capture was replaced
# by a live one whose extra usage is switched OFF while $100.55 of it is spent,
# and the new cases pin both halves of that — the gate that now shows it, and the
# never-enabled account that must still show nothing.
#
# Raised again on 2026-08-26 by Rounds polish: lib/polish (23) covers the runtime
# frame the polish prompt teaches and the distrustful parse around the answer, and
# routes-polish (15) covers the endpoint — including the CAGE itself, asserted off
# the argv a stub claude records, because `--tools ""` and `--setting-sources ""`
# going missing changes nothing anyone could see in a reply.
#
# Raised again on 2026-08-27 by scratchpads: lib/scratchpads (14) pins the naming
# rules and the two frames as literals, and routes-scratchpads (20) covers the
# CRUD, Main's protection, the revision guard and the two that matter most — a
# queued message carrying the page as it read when SEND was pressed rather than
# when the queue drains, and pages never entering the watch digest (lib/watch.js
# says exactly which facts belong in that hash and why; a page is written by the
# person holding the phone, so buzzing them about their own typing is the purest
# form of the noise it exists to avoid).
#
# Raised again on 2026-08-26 by device lifecycle: 19 covering the uninstall sweep
# and the registry on disk. The load-bearing ones are the negatives — that an
# outage mid-sweep is never read as a dead token (it would empty the registry in
# one pass, with no way back but reinstalling on every phone), and that
# `validate_only` sits BESIDE the message rather than inside it, where it would
# turn a daily probe into a real 3am push to every device.
